Q: How to pass data from Angular 9 UI to .Net 3.1 controller A: <p>In general, .NET try to use camelCase when you call/receive data.</p>
<p>So, you should use</p>
<pre><code>builder.Services.AddControllers().AddJsonOptions(options =&gt; {
    options.JsonSerializerOptions.PropertyNamingPolicy = null;
});

</code></pre>
<p>In order to use PascalCase (really this makes that not change in properties)</p>
<p>Tip: for Testing, you can define a function in your API like</p>
<pre><code>//NOT USE IN PRODUCTION, only for Test
[HttpPost]
public async Task&lt;object&gt; Post()
{
    using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(Request.Body))
    {
        var result = await reader.ReadToEndAsync();
        return result;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>See that no arguments in Post. In this way you can see the object you received</p>
<p>See that it's NOT RECOMENDED in production, in production you should use, like your code</p>
<pre><code>Post([FromBody]MyType value){...}

</code></pre>
<p>To be sure the function it's only &quot;reach&quot; if a well formed arguments are sending.</p>

Q: Is there a more succinct way to convert a String to a Set of Characters in Java? A: <h2>BitSet</h2>
<p>Mentioned is that <code>char</code> is not a perfect choice for what should be three byte ints, <em>code points</em>.</p>
<p>With that in mind (<code>char</code> not being semantical perfect), and optimal only for an ASCII/Latin range of chars, there is an other solution: <code>BitSet</code>.</p>
<pre><code>BitSet hx = new BitSet(1278);
hx.set('0', '9' + 1);
hx.set('A', 'F' + 1);
hx.set('a', 'f' + 1);

boolean containsCh = hx.get(ch);
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>Advantages: API with ranges, memory and speed performance.</li>
<li>Contraindication: when the set must contain high Unicode code points, but then <code>char</code> is not suited too.</li>
</ul>
